{
  "name": "Schrödinger Onsager clusters: shear layer",
  "description": "Defocusing Schrödinger (a superfluid in a box, g = −1): a row of 9 vortices spinning one way (top) and a row of 9 spinning the other (bottom): two quantum shear layers. Each row wobbles and rolls up (a quantum Kelvin-Helmholtz instability), and the vortices sort themselves into one large swirling cluster of each sign, which then wander round the box together (measured: 18 vortices, 2–3 lost at the walls; 82–95% keep a same-sign nearest neighbour through 60k steps). Onsager predicted in 1949 that vortices with this much energy cluster rather than mix (a negative-temperature state); it was first seen directly in Bose-Einstein condensates in 2019.",
